@.str.1369 = private unnamed_addr constant [17 x i8] c"HelpAssetCaching\00", align 1
@.str.1370 = private unnamed_addr constant [228 x i8] c"The '<rw>' part references code in the following table and should not be localized. The matching values \22read\22 \22write\22 and \22readwrite\22 are also fixed. After this block a table with each possible asset caching source is printed.\00", align 1
@.str.1371 = private unnamed_addr constant [728 x i8] c"**Experimental feature: this may change or be removed at any time**\0A\0Avcpkg can use mirrors to cache downloaded assets, ensuring continued operation even if the original source changes or disappears.\0A\0AAsset caching can be configured either by setting the environment variable X_VCPKG_ASSET_SOURCES to a semicolon-delimited list of sources or by passing a sequence of --x-asset-sources=<source> command line options. Command line sources are interpreted after environment sources. Commas, semicolons, and backticks can be escaped using backtick (`).\0A\0AThe <rw> optional parameter for certain strings controls how they will be accessed. It can be specified as \22read\22, \22write\22, or \22readwrite\22 and defaults to \22read\22.\0A\0AValid sources:\00", align 1
@.str.1372 = private unnamed_addr constant [22 x i8] c"HelpAssetCachingAzUrl\00", align 1
@.str.1373 = private unnamed_addr constant [134 x i8] c"This is printed as the 'definition' in a table for 'x-azurl,<url>[,<sas>[,<rw>]]', so <url>, <sas>, and <rw> should not be localized.\00", align 1
@.str.1374 = private unnamed_addr constant [323 x i8] c"Adds an Azure Blob Storage source, optionally using Shared Access Signature validation. URL should include the container path and be terminated with a trailing \22/\22. <sas>, if defined, should be prefixed with a \22?\22. Non-Azure servers will also work if they respond to GET and PUT requests of the form: \22<url><sha512><sas>\22.\00", align 1
@.str.1375 = private unnamed_addr constant [28 x i8] c"HelpAssetCachingBlockOrigin\00", align 1
@.str.1376 = private unnamed_addr constant [68 x i8] c"This is printed as the 'definition' in a table for 'x-block-origin'\00", align 1
@.str.1377 = private unnamed_addr constant [92 x i8] c"Disables fallback to the original URLs in case the mirror does not have the file available.\00", align 1
@.str.1378 = private unnamed_addr constant [23 x i8] c"HelpAssetCachingScript\00", align 1
@.str.1379 = private unnamed_addr constant [113 x i8] c"This is printed as the 'definition' in a table for 'x-script,<template>', so <template> should not be localized.\00", align 1
@.str.1380 = private unnamed_addr constant [585 x i8] c"Dispatches to an external tool to fetch the asset. Within the template, \22{{url}}\22 will be replaced by the original url, \22{{sha512}}\22 will be replaced by the SHA512 value, and \22{{dst}}\22 will be replaced by the output path to save to. These substitutions will all be properly shell escaped, so an example template would be: \22curl -L {{url}} --output {{dst}}\22. \22{{{{\22 will be replaced by \22}}\22 and \22}}}}\22 will be replaced by \22}}\22 to avoid expansion. Note that this will be executed inside the build environment, so the PATH and other environment variables will be modified by the triplet.\00", align 1
@.str.1381 = private unnamed_addr constant [18 x i8] c"HelpBinaryCaching\00", align 1
@.str.1382 = private unnamed_addr constant [180 x i8] c"The names in angle brackets like <rw> or in curly braces like {{sha512}} are 'code' and should not be localized. The matching values \22read\22 \22write\22 and \22readwrite\22 are also fixed.\00", align 1
@.str.1383 = private unnamed_addr constant [806 x i8] c"vcpkg can cache compiled packages to accelerate restoration on a single machine or across the network. By default, vcpkg will save builds to a local machine cache. This can be disabled by passing \22--binarysource=clear\22 as the last option on the command line.\0A\0ABinary caching can be further configured by either passing \22--binarysource=<source>\22 options to every command line or setting the `VCPKG_BINARY_SOURCES` environment variable to a set of sources (Example: \22<source>;<source>;...\22). Command line sources are interpreted after environment sources.\0A\0AThe \22<rw>\22 optional parameter for certain strings controls whether they will be consulted for downloading binaries and whether on-demand builds will be uploaded to that remote. It can be specified as \22read\22, \22write\22, or \22readwrite\22.\0A\0AGeneral sources:\00", align 1
@.str.1384 = private unnamed_addr constant [21 x i8] c"HelpBinaryCachingAws\00", align 1
